# Task 22. Without this, `COPY . .` in the builder stage pulls the entire
# `target/` directory (multiple GB after a local build) and the generated
# scratch dirs into the build context, which is slow at best and, on a machine
# with a warm cargo cache, large enough to make `docker build` look hung.
/target
/.e2e
/.demo
/.engine
/.git
/upstream
**/*.tar.gz

# Task 9, carried from Task 8b's review. `COPY . .` is the last thing before
# `RUN cargo build` (Dockerfile:144-151), so ANY file that reaches the build
# context invalidates the cargo layer — including the two files that describe
# the build itself. Editing a comment in the Dockerfile therefore re-ran a
# 92-second release compile that could not possibly differ. Neither file is
# read from inside the builder stage: BuildKit takes the Dockerfile through the
# frontend, not through the context tar, so excluding it here changes what
# `COPY . .` copies and nothing else.
/Dockerfile
/.dockerignore

# Task 23, for exactly the reason above. `Dockerfile.weirkeeper` is the second
# Dockerfile in this tree and is taken through BuildKit's frontend, not through
# the context tar, so excluding it here changes what `COPY . .` copies and
# nothing else — and stops an edit to ITS comments invalidating the cargo layer
# of the OTHER image's build.
/Dockerfile.weirkeeper

# D0 stage 7, for exactly the reason above. `Dockerfile.console` is the fourth
# Dockerfile in this tree and is taken through BuildKit's frontend, not through
# the context tar, so excluding it here changes what `COPY . .` copies and
# nothing else — and stops an edit to ITS comments invalidating the cargo layer
# of the other two compiling builds.
/Dockerfile.console
